this may look like something out of a  horror film but it's actually a glass  worm  it's the larval form of a very common  lake insect called the phantom itch and  it's evolved kind of a horrific way to  catch a zooplankton these centimeter are  so long worms ambush their prey with  long articulated bristles that rapidly  unfurl into a catching basket recently  researchers discovered exactly how this  capture mechanism works they were using  high-speed video to study the  interactions between glass worms and one  of its main prey items water fleas also  known as Daphnia they found that many  bristles prevent the prey from escaping  including long upper lip hairs that fan  out sideways during the glass worm  attack other researchers had doubted  that these lip hairs could move because  they lack muscle but they might be  powered by the pressure of internal  fluids these videos shot at up to 8,000  frames per second also allowed the  researchers to time the attack the  bristles reached their target in just 14  milliseconds one of the fastest known  strikes out there for comparison the  mantis shrimp takes just four to eight  milliseconds the record-holders trap-jaw  ant's and trap jaws spiders attack in  less than one millisecond after the  glass worm has its prey safely and  snared it needs around 40 more  milliseconds to retract and then swallow  the prey but as you can see from this  real-time footage  [Music]  that's still pretty fast  [Music]  [Music]
 

